  "answer": "### Short Answer\nThe VAT rate on the importation of goods used to manufacture export products is 12%. This is based on the total value used by the Bureau of Customs in determining tariff and customs duties, plus any applicable customs duties and excise taxes.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe controlling authority for this VAT rate is found in Section 107 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that there shall be levied, assessed, and collected a value-added tax equivalent to twelve percent (12%) on every importation of goods. This is further supported by Revenue Regulations No. 16-2005.\n\n### Interpretation\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor businesses importing goods intended for the manufacture of export products, the 12% VAT on importation is a significant cost factor that must be accounted for in pricing and financial planning. However, if these goods are used in the production of zero-rated export sales, the business may be eligible to claim input tax credits or refunds on the VAT paid, effectively mitigating the tax burden associated with the importation.",
